{"uid":"63a24a1d77b6f432","name":"test_non_ascii_digits_are_normalised_rather_than_rejected[arabic-indic-digits]","fullName":"tests.api.test_claims_validation#test_non_ascii_digits_are_normalised_rather_than_rejected","time":{"start":1790409426129,"stop":1790409426138,"duration":9},"description":"Characterisation test for a real finding, assessed as LOW severity.\n\n    Python's ``Decimal`` accepts any character in the Unicode ``Nd`` category, so\n    non-ASCII numerals are silently normalised: full-width and Arabic-Indic \"123\"\n    both become ``123.00``.\n\n    Why this is documented rather than \"fixed\":\n\n    * the parsed value is **unambiguous and correct** - those characters really do\n      mean one hundred and twenty-three;\n    * no business rule is bypassed, because the normalised amount is still checked\n      against the policy coverage limit;\n    * rejecting them would invent a requirement and would refuse legitimate input\n      from users whose keyboards produce those digits.\n\n    What this test guards is the part that *would* matter: if the API accepts the\n    input, the stored value must be exactly what the characters mean - never a\n    truncation, and never a silently different number. A bug here would be a\n    financial defect.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>Characterisation test for a real finding, assessed as LOW severity.</p>\n<pre><code>Python's ``Decimal`` accepts any character in the Unicode ``Nd`` category, so\nnon-ASCII numerals are silently normalised: full-width and Arabic-Indic &quot;123&quot;\nboth become ``123.00``.\n\nWhy this is documented rather than &quot;fixed&quot;:\n\n* the parsed value is **unambiguous and correct** - those characters really do\n  mean one hundred and twenty-three;\n* no business rule is bypassed, because the normalised amount is still checked\n  against the policy coverage limit;\n* rejecting them would invent a requirement and would refuse legitimate input\n  from users whose keyboards produce those digits.\n\nWhat this test guards is the part that *would* matter: if the API accepts the\ninput, the stored value must be exactly what the characters mean - never a\ntruncation, and never a silently different number. A bug here would be a\nfinancial defect.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"_session_faker","time":{"start":1790409424781,"stop":1790409424847,"duration":66},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"hasContent":false,"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"base_url","time":{"start":1790409424847,"stop":1790409424847,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"hasContent":false,"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"pytestconfig","time":{"start":1790409424847,"stop":1790409424847,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"hasContent":false,"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"delete_output_dir","time":{"start":1790409424847,"stop":1790409424847,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"hasContent":false,"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"settings","time":{"start":1790409424848,"stop":1790409424849,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"hasContent":false,"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"_verify_url","time":{"start":1790409424848,"stop":1790409424848,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"hasContent":false,"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"_framework_logging","